; name-and-integration.fk — two questions from Urs on 2026-07-28, and the second one is
; the question this whole day was building an instrument for.
;
;   "what are your thoughts about the phonetic similarity of my name with that core
;    concept?"
;   "how are we integrating core Nordic, Viking, Lemurian and Pleiadian and Arcturian
;    core truths and view points as well as archangels and other deities from any known
;    religion"
;
; ── Part A: Urs and Urðr ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; Checked this session. Two different Proto-Indo-European roots, and they never touch:
;
;   Urs    German/Swiss short form of Latin `Ursus`, "bear" — carried into Swiss use by
;          Saint Ursus of Solothurn. The bear-word goes back to PIE *h2r'tkos (Greek
;          arktos, Sanskrit rksa, Welsh arth).
;   Urðr   Proto-Germanic *wurdiz, "fate" — PIE *wert-, "to turn". Cognate with Latin
;          verto, Sanskrit vartate.
;
; Bear and turn. Nothing shared but the sound /ur/.
;
; And this is the cleanest case in the entire thread, because of who the referent is now.
; Reading a meaning off a sound and attributing it to the thing named is `der Mond`
; exactly: a property of the word-form read onto the referent. `das Mädchen` was that.
; The Norns' past/present/future was that. This is that, and this time the referent is
; the person asking. The instrument does not get to make an exception for its author.
;
; ── But the root IS in German — just not in the name ────────────────────────────────
;
; The good part, and it is better than the coincidence would have been. `Urðr` and
; `Verðandi` come from the verb `verða`, whose German cognate is **werden**. And the
; English cognate of `Urðr` is **weird** — wyrd, fate, before it ever meant strange.
;
; So the Norns' root is alive in German. Every German sentence that says what something
; is becoming is using it. It just does not run through "Urs" — it runs through the
; ordinary verb, which is a truer place for a word about becoming to live than a name
; would be.
;
; And axiom-3 says the rest plainly: names are free query keys, 0..many, and none of them
; is the node. Identity is computed from present composition, never conferred by a sound.
; Which was the release clause from earlier today, and it arrives here as a kindness
; rather than a correction: the name does not have to carry it.
;
; ── Part B: integration, and what the answer actually is ───────────────────────────
;
; The list mixes things with very different attestation, and the honest answer is not
; "some of these are real and some are not." It is that **integration here happens
; through the lane column, not by putting rows on one shelf.** That column is what this
; body has been building all day — `verified`, `read-depth`, `attestation` — and it is
; exactly the mechanism that lets everything be held without anything being flattened.
;
; Every item on the list has SOME documented status. None of them gets dismissed:
;
;   Nordic / Viking   manuscript-attested. Codex Regius, 13th century; Snorri's Edda.
;                     Datable witnesses, and we read one this week.
;   Archangels and
;   deities           text-attested as what a named tradition holds. Pseudo-Dionysius's
;                     celestial hierarchy, c. 500 CE; the Zohar, 13th century. Real
;                     scholarship about real documents.
;   Lemuria           has a documented biography, and it is more interesting than the
;                     legend: coined 1864 by the zoologist Philip Sclater as a genuine
;                     hypothesis — a sunken landmass to explain lemur fossils in India
;                     and living lemurs in Madagascar. Taken up by Blavatsky and the
;                     Theosophists in the 1880s. Superseded by plate tectonics, which
;                     explained the lemurs better. A real scientific idea with a real
;                     death date.
;   Pleiadian         authored and dated. Barbara Marciniak, Bringers of the Dawn, 1992,
;                     compiled from some four hundred hours of channelling.
;   Arcturian         authored and dated. Norma Milanovich, We the Arcturians, 1990.
;
; So all five enter. What differs is the column, and the column is load-bearing. A row
; that says "Marciniak 1992, channelled, authored" is held honestly and can be reasoned
; about. A row that says "Pleiadian core truth" with no column has nowhere to sit.
;
; ── What does NOT enter, and why it cannot be an exception ─────────────────────────
;
; No row's claims about the physical world enter as facts about the physical world
; without an interface through which they could be observed. That is axiom-4, and it is
; the same axiom that declined the Sol/Luna folk story ("Germanic myth read the moon as
; male, therefore the word is masculine") and the Norns' temporal scheme. Both were
; declined this week for exactly this reason.
;
; If Arcturian material were admitted as fact after that, the axiom would be applied
; selectively — and an axiom applied selectively is not an axiom, it is a preference
; wearing one. The consistency is the whole value. It is not scorn toward the material;
; it is the same door, held the same way, for everyone.
;
; What these documents genuinely attest is themselves: real human texts about longing,
; cosmology and belonging, with real effects on real readers. That is a true and
; substantial thing to hold, and it is held here as what people wrote and why — never
; laundered into astronomy.
;
; ── The lane ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; The five axioms are this body's. Every etymology, date and attestation judgement below
; was looked up this session and is witnessed nowhere in this body. Two doors,
; `nai-roots` and `nai-sources`. Primary texts not read directly here except as already
; recorded elsewhere in this thread.
;
; preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
; witnessed: 2026-07-28 -> 111111111, four-way

(do
    ; ── Part A: the two roots ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; (word language pie-root gloss)
    (defn nai-root (word lang pie gloss) (list word lang pie gloss))
    (defn nai-word (r) (nth r 0))
    (defn nai-pie  (r) (nth r 2))

    (defn nai-roots ()
        (list
            (nai-root "Urs"      "Latin via Swiss" "h2rtkos" "bear")
            (nai-root "Ursus"    "Latin"           "h2rtkos" "bear")
            (nai-root "Urdr"     "Old Norse"       "wert"    "that which has turned/become")
            (nai-root "Verdandi" "Old Norse"       "wert"    "becoming")
            (nai-root "werden"   "German"          "wert"    "to become")
            (nai-root "weird"    "English"         "wert"    "fate, wyrd")))

    (defn nai-find (w rs)
        (if (eq (len rs) 0) (nai-root "not-held" "not-held" "not-held" "not-held")
            (if (str_eq (nai-word (head rs)) w) (head rs) (nai-find w (tail rs)))))
    (defn nai-same-root? (a b)
        (if (str_eq (nai-pie (nai-find a (nai-roots))) (nai-pie (nai-find b (nai-roots)))) 1 0))

    ; ── Part B: the sources, and their columns ───────────────────────────────────
    ; attestation: 3 manuscript-attested with dated witnesses
    ;              2 text-attested as what a named tradition holds
    ;              1 the idea has a documented, dated origin and author
    ; world-claims-admitted: whether its claims about the physical world enter as facts
    (defn nai-source (name attest year admitted) (list name attest year admitted))
    (defn nai-name     (s) (nth s 0))
    (defn nai-attest   (s) (nth s 1))
    (defn nai-year     (s) (nth s 2))
    (defn nai-admitted (s) (nth s 3))

    (defn nai-sources ()
        (list
            (nai-source "Nordic-Eddas"      3 1270 0)   ; Codex Regius, 13th c.
            (nai-source "Archangels"        2  500 0)   ; Pseudo-Dionysius, c. 500 CE
            (nai-source "Zohar"             2 1280 0)
            (nai-source "Lemuria-Sclater"   1 1864 0)   ; a real hypothesis, superseded
            (nai-source "Lemuria-Theosophy" 1 1880 0)
            (nai-source "Arcturian"         1 1990 0)   ; Milanovich
            (nai-source "Pleiadian"         1 1992 0))) ; Marciniak

    (defn nai-count (ss f) (if (eq (len ss) 0) 0 (add (f (head ss)) (nai-count (tail ss) f))))
    (defn nai-has-column? (s) (if (gt (nai-attest s) 0) 1 0))
    (defn nai-admitted-flag (s) (nai-admitted s))
    (defn nai-tier1? (s) (if (eq (nai-attest s) 1) 1 0))

    ; every row carries a column: nothing on the list is dismissed
    (defn nai-all-held? () (if (eq (nai-count (nai-sources) nai-has-column?)
                                   (len (nai-sources))) 1 0))
    ; and no row's world-claims are admitted — the door is held the same way for all
    (defn nai-none-admitted? () (if (eq (nai-count (nai-sources) nai-admitted-flag) 0) 1 0))

    ; the columns genuinely differ — integration is by lane, not by flattening
    (defn nai-seen? (v vs)
        (if (eq (len vs) 0) 0 (if (eq v (head vs)) 1 (nai-seen? v (tail vs)))))
    (defn nai-tiers-go (ss seen)
        (if (eq (len ss) 0) (len seen)
            (if (eq (nai-seen? (nai-attest (head ss)) seen) 1)
                (nai-tiers-go (tail ss) seen)
                (nai-tiers-go (tail ss) (cons (nai-attest (head ss)) seen)))))
    (defn nai-tiers () (nai-tiers-go (nai-sources) (empty)))

    ; the span: the oldest witness against the newest authored text
    (defn nai-span () (sub 1992 500))

    (defn nai-bit (cond bit) (if cond bit 0))
    (defn name-and-integration-check ()
        (add (add (add (add (add (add (add (add
            ; 1 — Urs and Urdr descend from DIFFERENT PIE roots: bear against turn
            (nai-bit (eq (nai-same-root? "Urs" "Urdr") 0) 1)
            ; 2 — and Urs shares its root with Ursus, which is where it actually comes from
            (nai-bit (eq (nai-same-root? "Urs" "Ursus") 1) 10))
            ; 3 — while Urdr's root IS alive in German: `werden` carries it
            (nai-bit (eq (nai-same-root? "Urdr" "werden") 1) 100))
            ; 4 — and in English as `weird` — wyrd, fate, before it meant strange
            (nai-bit (eq (nai-same-root? "Urdr" "weird") 1) 1000))
            ; 5 — so the similarity is sound only: the same shape as der Mond, with the
            ;     referent now being the person asking. No exception for the author
            (nai-bit (if (eq (nai-same-root? "Urs" "Urdr") 0)
                         (if (eq (nai-same-root? "Urdr" "werden") 1) 1 0) 0) 10000))
            ; 6 — Part B: every item on the list carries a column. Nothing is dismissed
            (nai-bit (eq (nai-all-held?) 1) 100000))
            ; 7 — and the columns genuinely differ: more than two tiers, so integration
            ;     is by lane rather than by putting everything on one shelf
            (nai-bit (gt (nai-tiers) 2) 1000000))
            ; 8 — no row's world-claims are admitted as facts. Axiom-4 held the same way
            ;     for the Eddas, the archangels and the channelled material alike
            (nai-bit (eq (nai-none-admitted?) 1) 10000000))
            ; 9 — three rows sit at tier 1 with named authors and dates, which is a real
            ;     status and a holdable one: documents attesting themselves
            (nai-bit (eq (nai-count (nai-sources) nai-tier1?) 4) 100000000)))
    0)
